.TH SOURCEKNOCK 1 "2026-11-02" "sourceknock 1.1" "sourceknock"
.SH NAME
sourceknock \- source-port port-knocking daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sourceknock
.RB [ \-t ]
.RI [ CONFIGFILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sourceknock
is a port-knocking daemon that unlocks a hidden service port by watching the
.B source ports
of incoming packets, rather than the destination ports that
traditional knocking encodes the secret in.
.PP
Standard port knocking puts the knock sequence in the
.I destination
port, so a scanner watching destination ports sees a client stepping through a
fixed list of ports and can fingerprint it.
.B sourceknock
keeps the destination port fixed and varies the
.I source
port instead, so the knock blends in with ordinary traffic.
.PP
How it works:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
The daemon captures packets on an interface using an
.B AF_PACKET
raw socket (requires root or
.B CAP_NET_RAW ).
.IP \(bu 2
For each packet it decodes the source port and source IP from both IPv4 and
IPv6 frames (IPv6 extension headers are walked, bounded to 8 hops).
.IP \(bu 2
A per-client state machine watches the stream of source ports and waits for the
configured ordered sequence to arrive within the timing window.
.IP \(bu 2
On a successful knock the daemon programs
.B nftables
to let that client's source IP reach the hidden service port, then closes it
again after a fixed TTL or on the first connection.
.RE
.PP
.B Threat model.
Source ports are trivially spoofable, like any L4 header field.
This gives
.B sourceknock
stealth and obfuscation (the attack surface stays invisible; noisy scans look
normal) but
.B not
authentication. Rely on your service's own authentication (SSH keys, passwords)
for real security; do not treat
.B sourceknock
as the only control.
.SH ARGUMENTS
The daemon takes a single optional argument: the path to its configuration
file. If omitted it defaults to
.BI /etc/sourceknock/sourceknock.conf .
See
.IR CONFIGURATION
below and the samples in
.BR examples/ .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-t
Check the configuration and exit: 0 if the daemon would accept it, 1 with the
reason if not. Needs no privileges and does not touch nftables. Run it before
restarting the daemon: a daemon that refuses a new config leaves the previous
run's tables closing the ports.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration (and any
.BR secret_file ).
A config that doesn't parse or can't be applied is refused and the running one
kept; a good one is applied without dropping clients already let in.
.B systemctl reload
sends this.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop: remove every allowed client and exit. The tables stay, so the ports stay
closed.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is INI-style (knockd-flavoured) with a
.B [global]
section and one
.B [trigger:NAME]
section per hidden service. The minimum per trigger is a
.B port
and a
.BR sequence ;
the fields are:
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
.B service
Human-readable name; also used to namespace the nftables table
(``inet sourceknock_<service>'').
.IP \(bu 2
.B port
The hidden service port(s) to open. Accepts a single port, a comma-separated
list, or an inclusive range, e.g.
.BI 22 ,
.BI "80, 443" ,
or
.BI "8000-8002" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B protocol
.BI tcp
or
.BI udp .
.IP \(bu 2
.B sequence
The ordered source-port knock, e.g.
.BI 7000,8000,9000 .
.IP \(bu 2
.B window
Seconds within which the whole sequence must complete (default 5).
.IP \(bu 2
.B mode
One of
.BI fixed_ttl ,
.BI close_on_connect ,
or
.BI permanent .
.IP \(bu 2
.B duration
Seconds the port stays open with
.BI fixed_ttl
(default 30).
.IP \(bu 2
.B fallback_ttl
Safety maximum open time for
.BI close_on_connect
so a client that never connects cannot leave the port open forever (default 300).
Enforced by the kernel as the nft element timeout, so it holds even if the
daemon is not running.
.IP \(bu 2
.B admin_sequence
Optional revoke knock for
.BI permanent
mode; when it completes the client's IP is removed and the port closes again.
.IP \(bu 2
.B knock_port
Optional destination port. When set, only frames sent to this port count
toward the knock, so other traffic from the client (or replies to the
server's own outbound connections) can neither advance nor reset it. Pick a
port nothing listens on and that is not a service port.
.IP \(bu 2
.B secret_file
Instead of
.BR sequence :
a file holding a shared secret (at least 16 bytes). The sequence is then 4
ports derived from HMAC-SHA256 of the secret and the 30-second time step,
like a 2FA code. The previous, current and next step are accepted (clocks may
differ by about 30 seconds; keep them synced), and each step's sequence opens
the port only once, so a seen knock can't be replayed. The file must have
mode 0600 (owned by the daemon's user). If the group or other users can read
it, the daemon logs a warning at start, at reload and in
.BR "\-t" ,
and still uses the key. Knock with
.BR "sourceknock-knock -K" ;
.B \-r
sends the revoke knock.
.IP \(bu 2
.B max_allowed
Optional. The most client addresses let in at once, per family (default 256).
.IP \(bu 2
.B interface
Optional. The interface this trigger reads knocks from and closes its ports
on; defaults to the
.B [global]
.BR interface .
The drop rules match only packets arriving on it, so the same port over any
other interface (a VPN, loopback) is left alone. One daemon can guard
services on several interfaces this way. An interface that is down, deleted
or not there yet does not stop the daemon: that trigger's ports stay closed
and knocks are read there again once it is up.
.IP \(bu 2
.B close_on
Optional:
.B interface
or
.BR all .
Where the drop rules apply; defaults to the
.B [global]
.BR close_on ,
and the built-in default is
.BR interface .
With
.B interface
the drop rules match only the trigger's interface, so the port stays open on
the host's other interfaces. At start, at reload and in
.B \-t
the daemon logs one warning for each such trigger. It names the other
interfaces (not
.BR lo )
and says to set
.BR "close_on = all" .
With
.B all
the drop rules have no interface match, so the port is closed on every
interface, including loopback. Knocks are still read only on the trigger's
interface. Two triggers may not manage the same port and protocol when either
one uses
.BR all .
.RE
.PP
Several daemons may run on one host, each with its own configuration.
Every table carries the absolute path of its configuration as an nft table
comment (``sourceknock:<path>''). On start and on reload the daemon deletes
every ``inet sourceknock_*'' table that is not in its configuration and has its
own mark, so a renamed or removed trigger does not leave its port closed. A
table with no mark, or with the mark of another configuration, is never
deleted: the daemon logs a warning that names it. The mark needs nftables with
table comments (tested with nft 1.0.9, Linux 5.10 or later) and a path of at
most 116 characters. A table made by an earlier version has no mark and is
kept. Two triggers may not manage
the same port and protocol on the same interface, or on any interfaces when
either one uses
.BR "close_on = all" .
.SH MODES
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
.B fixed_ttl \- keep the port open for
.BI duration
seconds, then close.
.IP \(bu 2
.B close_on_connect \- close as soon as the client makes a connection, with
.BI fallback_ttl
as a safety net.
.IP
For TCP the first new connection (a SYN) to a service port closes the port;
packets of a session that is already open do not. For UDP there is no handshake, so the first subsequent UDP packet to a
service port closes it; the arming knock itself does not close the port the
instant it opens.
.IP \(bu 2
.B permanent \- keep the port open with no timeout. Pair with
.BI admin_sequence
to revoke.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Open SSH (port 22) for 30 seconds after a knock:
.IP
.BR "sudo sourceknock /etc/sourceknock/sourceknock.conf" ""
.PP
Knock from a client with
.BR sourceknock-knock (1),
which sends each step from the right SOURCE port, in order, then connect:
.IP
.nf
sourceknock-knock server 7000,8000,9000
ssh user@server
.fi
.PP
Run under systemd (installs the binary and units, then reloads systemd):
.IP
.nf
sudo make install
sudo useradd --system --user-group --home-dir /var/lib/sourceknock \
    --no-create-home sourceknock
sudo systemctl enable --now sourceknock
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /etc/sourceknock/sourceknock.conf
Default configuration file.
.TP
.BI /usr/local/bin/sourceknock
Installed daemon binary (see
.BR make install ).
.TP
.BI /usr/local/bin/sourceknock-knock
Installed knock client; see
.BR sourceknock-knock (1).
.TP
.BI /etc/systemd/system/sourceknock.service
Default systemd unit.
.TP
.BI /etc/systemd/system/sourceknock@.service
Per-instance systemd template unit.
.SH DEBUGGING
To inspect what the daemon has programmed, list the nftables set for a
service. With a trigger named
.BI ssh :
.IP
.BR "nft list set inet sourceknock_ssh allowed_v4" ""
.PP
Common problems (permissions, missing/old nftables, IPv6 quirks) are covered in
the project's
.B CONTRIBUTING.md
and the Troubleshooting section of the README.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.RS 4
.IP \(bu 2
.B 0
The daemon ran until signalled.
.IP \(bu 2
.B 1
Configuration error, an interface whose link type can't be parsed, nftables
could not be programmed (missing nft or
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN ),
or capture open failure (usually missing privileges). Any tables already
programmed are removed again, so a failed start leaves the host as it was.
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (8),
.BR nft (8),
.BR sourceknock-knock (1)
.PP
